# AutoClip

**Open-source, local-first AI video clipper.** Long video in → ranked, caption-burned, speaker-tracked 9:16 clips out.

[![CI](https://github.com/Kamil-Krawiec/autoclip/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/Kamil-Krawiec/autoclip/actions/workflows/ci.yml)
[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/license-MIT-blue.svg)](LICENSE)
[![Python 3.11 | 3.12](https://img.shields.io/badge/python-3.11%20%7C%203.12-blue.svg)](https://www.python.org/)

Paste a YouTube link or drop a file. AutoClip transcribes it, uses an LLM to find the moments worth clipping, reframes them to vertical while tracking whoever is speaking, burns in animated captions, and exports platform-ready MP4s.

No accounts. No uploads to anyone's servers unless you opt into the YouTube
**Publish** flow with your own OAuth client. No watermarks. No subscription.

---

## Why this exists

Existing open-source clippers stop at "works on my machine" — no UI, janky reframing, ugly captions. AutoClip ships the whole loop: ingest → clips → review → export.

Two ways to run it:

- **Fully local** — Whisper + Ollama. Nothing leaves your machine, no API costs.
- **Bring your own key** — Anthropic, OpenAI, Gemini, or any OpenAI-compatible endpoint (OpenRouter, Groq, DeepSeek, LM Studio) for better clip selection.

Only transcript *text* is ever sent to a provider — never video or audio. With Ollama, nothing is sent at all.

## Status

Working end to end: ingest, transcription, highlight detection across four providers, speaker-tracked reframing, four caption styles, export at three aspect ratios, and the full review UI. Verified on real footage — see [what "verified" means](#what-has-and-hasnt-been-verified).

Pre-1.0. The reframe quality bar hasn't been validated against a fixed golden set yet, which is the gate for tagging v0.1.0.

An optional **Publish** flow adds AI title/description, a retryable thumbnail,
a tcap-compatible still, and direct YouTube upload with your own OAuth client.

## Requirements

| | |
|---|---|
| **Python** | 3.11 or 3.12 — **not 3.13.** MediaPipe publishes no 3.13 wheels and the reframe stage needs it. |
| **ffmpeg** | A *full* build with `libass` and `libx264`. Both `ffmpeg` and `ffprobe` on PATH. See below — the default package is the wrong one on macOS and Windows. |
| **Node** | 20+, to build the UI. Not needed at runtime. |
| **GPU** | Optional. NVIDIA or Apple Silicon speeds up transcription several-fold; CPU works, just slower. |

`autoclip doctor` checks all of this and tells you exactly what to fix. Run it before anything else.

### Installing ffmpeg correctly

Captions are burned in with **libass**, and on two platforms the obvious package doesn't include it. It installs cleanly, then fails to burn a single caption.

**macOS** — Homebrew split the formula; the plain one is a reduced build:

```bash
brew install ffmpeg-full
```

If you already installed the plain one: `brew unlink ffmpeg && brew link --force --overwrite ffmpeg-full`.

**Windows** — install the *full* build, not `Gyan.FFmpeg.Essentials`:

```bash
winget install Gyan.FFmpeg
```

**Linux** — the distro package is fine:

```bash
sudo apt install ffmpeg
```

## Quickstart

```bash
git clone https://github.com/Kamil-Krawiec/autoclip.git
```

```bash
cd autoclip && uv venv --python 3.11 && uv pip install -e ".[dev]"
```

```bash
cd frontend && npm install && npm run build && cd ..
```

```bash
autoclip doctor
```

```bash
autoclip serve
```

That opens `http://localhost:8000`. If you don't use [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/), a plain `python -m venv .venv` and `pip install -e ".[dev]"` works the same way.

### Add a provider

Clip selection needs a language model. Either paste a key in **Settings → Keys**, or:

```bash
autoclip config set-secret anthropic
```

Keys go into your OS keyring — Credential Manager, Keychain, or Secret Service — never into a config file. If no keyring backend exists, AutoClip falls back to a file **and says so**, in the UI and in `doctor`.

For a fully local setup, install [Ollama](https://ollama.com) and pull a model instead:

```bash
ollama pull llama3.1:8b
```

Clip quality tracks model quality closely. A 7B model returns valid JSON full of mediocre picks; a frontier model is noticeably better at spotting a real hook. That's the honest trade for running offline.

### Optional extras

```bash
uv pip install -e ".[gpu]"
```

CUDA runtime libraries for NVIDIA GPUs. Install these if `doctor` reports that CTranslate2 can't see your GPU — pip puts them somewhere the OS loader doesn't search, and AutoClip registers the location itself. On macOS this deliberately installs nothing: there's no CUDA there, and Apple Silicon takes the CPU path.

```bash
uv pip install -e ".[diarization]"
```

Speaker diarization via WhisperX — what lets the reframe stage cut to whoever is talking in a multi-person video. Pulls PyTorch (large), and needs a HuggingFace token plus acceptance of the gated pyannote model licences.

### Docker

The guaranteed path — ffmpeg, Python version, and fonts all pinned:

```bash
docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml up --build
```

```bash
docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml --profile gpu up --build
```

## Using it

Everything the UI does is also on the CLI:

| command | does |
|---|---|
| `autoclip doctor` | check this machine and explain what's missing |
| `autoclip serve` | start the web app |
| `autoclip clip <url\|file>` | run the whole pipeline and export |
| `autoclip jobs` | list recent jobs |
| `autoclip providers` | check which providers are reachable |
| `autoclip styles` | list caption presets |
| `autoclip publish auth` | authorize YouTube uploads (one-time) |
| `autoclip config show` | print settings |
| `autoclip update-ytdlp` | update yt-dlp after a YouTube change |

### Upload and reuse

File uploads show transferred bytes and progress, then **Validating media** while
the server checks the file. **Cancel** stops a transfer; retrying sends the file
again. Once validation starts, the server can finish saving the source even if
you leave the page. Transfers are limited to 8 GiB including multipart
overhead. Choose **Existing source → Process again** to skip uploading a saved
file, or **Open clips** to continue editing a completed analysis.

**Server media library** browses the read-only `/media` mount. With the supplied
Compose file, place media under `docker/media/`, choose a folder and file, then
**Process file**. AutoClip validates and uses the original file without copying
it. Keep it in place for processing, preview and export. Symlinks are excluded;
uploaded files remain managed copies in `/data`.

### Publish to YouTube

The **Publish** page in the top navigation turns a finished export into a
YouTube upload. It is opt-in: nothing leaves your machine until you press
**Publish**. The page lists ready exports (9:16 by default), generates an
editable Polish title, description and tags from the clip transcript, generates
a 9:16 thumbnail, and lets you retry the image with a new prompt. The model is
free to propose the wording and structure; there is no required keyword count
or hashtag format. On upload, AutoClip appends the still to the end of the MP4
(the same tcap-compatible step
used by the standalone pipeline), uploads with the YouTube Data API, and
best-effort sets the custom thumbnail.

#### Required secrets

| Secret | Where it goes | Used for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| OpenAI API key (`sk-...`) | **Settings → Keys → OpenAI**, or `AUTOCLIP_OPENAI_KEY` | metadata generation, default `gpt-6-luna` |
| OpenAI Images API key (`sk-...`) | **Settings → Keys → OpenAI Images**, or `AUTOCLIP_OPENAI_IMAGE_KEY` | thumbnail generation |
| YouTube OAuth client JSON | `$AUTOCLIP_HOME/secrets/youtube_client_secret.json` | one-time consent and token refresh |
| YouTube refresh token JSON | `$AUTOCLIP_HOME/secrets/youtube_token.json` (written by `autoclip publish auth`) | uploads without a browser |

`$AUTOCLIP_HOME` is `~/.autoclip` by default and `/data` in Docker. Both JSON
files must be readable only by you (`chmod 600` on Linux/macOS; restrict the
file's ACL on Windows). They are ignored by Git — never commit them.

In Docker, the easiest path for the two OpenAI keys is **Settings → Keys**
(they persist on `/data`). If you prefer environment variables, put them in an
untracked `.env` and pass them through `docker/compose.yaml` as
`AUTOCLIP_OPENAI_KEY` and `AUTOCLIP_OPENAI_IMAGE_KEY`.

Publish metadata defaults to OpenAI **`gpt-6-luna`**. You can switch it to the
active provider (or another model) in **Settings → Publishing**. The OpenAI
Images key is separate: thumbnails always use the Images API, so add an image
key even if clips are selected with Anthropic, Gemini or Ollama. You can paste
the same `sk-...` value under both keys; the separate name keeps an
OpenRouter/DeepSeek key from being sent to OpenAI Images. If you switch Publish
metadata to the active provider, that provider's existing key is used instead.

#### Google Cloud OAuth (once)

1. Create or select a project at <https://console.cloud.google.com/>.
2. **APIs & Services → Library → YouTube Data API v3 → Enable**.
3. **OAuth consent screen**: User type **External**, fill in the app name and
   support email, and add your Google account under **Test users**. In
   **Testing** mode Google issues refresh tokens that expire after **7 days**;
   either re-run `autoclip publish auth` weekly or publish the OAuth app.
4. **Credentials → Create credentials → OAuth client ID → Desktop app**.
5. Download the JSON and save it as:

   ```bash
   mkdir -p ~/.autoclip/secrets
   mv ~/Downloads/client_secret_*.json \
      ~/.autoclip/secrets/youtube_client_secret.json
   chmod 600 ~/.autoclip/secrets/youtube_client_secret.json
   ```

The only scope requested is
`https://www.googleapis.com/auth/youtube.upload`. YouTube also accepts that
scope for `thumbnails.set`, so no extra consent screen is needed.

#### Authorize once

On a machine with a browser:

```bash
autoclip publish auth
```

This opens Google's consent page and writes
`~/.autoclip/secrets/youtube_token.json`. To verify the token can refresh
without uploading anything:

```bash
autoclip publish auth --check
```

In Docker, run the auth command in a one-off container with port 8765
published. The command binds `0.0.0.0` but redirects to `localhost`, so the
browser on the host can complete the flow:

```bash
docker compose run --rm -p 8765:8765 autoclip \
  autoclip publish auth --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8765
```

If that is inconvenient, authorize on the host and copy both files into the
running container's `/data` volume:

```bash
docker compose cp ~/.autoclip/secrets/youtube_client_secret.json \
  autoclip:/data/secrets/youtube_client_secret.json
docker compose cp ~/.autoclip/secrets/youtube_token.json \
  autoclip:/data/secrets/youtube_token.json
```

#### Use it

1. Export a clip from **Review**. It appears under **Publish**.
2. Open it, press **Generate title & description**, and edit the result. The
   proposal is a YouTube title for this Short, not necessarily a copy of the
   internal clip title.
3. Press **Generate thumbnail**. If you do not like it, edit the prompt and
   press **Retry with new prompt**. The previous image stays until a new one
   succeeds. The prompt tells the model to keep the main subject and all text
   at least ~8–10% away from every edge; the preview shows a safe-area guide.
4. Choose privacy (**Private** by default), press **Publish**, and wait for the
   status to become **Published**. The YouTube link appears when it finishes.

**Private** is the default. Privacy is set at upload; change it later in
YouTube Studio if needed. A **Public** upload goes live immediately.

#### Limits and caveats

- The default YouTube API quota allows **100 `videos.insert` calls per day**;
  `thumbnails.set` costs 50 units from the daily 10,000-unit pool. Quotas reset
  at midnight US Pacific.
- If YouTube rejects the custom thumbnail (for example, the channel is not
  eligible), the video upload still succeeds. The tcap still remains at the end
  of the MP4 and can be selected in YouTube Studio.
- Uploading runs as a background task. You can leave the page; reopening the
  publication shows the result.
- The title is limited to 100 characters, the description to 5,000 bytes, and
  tags to 500 characters in total. These are YouTube limits, not editorial
  rules; AutoClip does not require a keyword count, hashtag suffix or fixed
  description layout.
- The thumbnail prompt is open-ended apart from one visual rule: keep the main
  subject and text away from the edges. If the generated image needs resizing,
  AutoClip pads it rather than cropping the safe area.
- Thumbnail generation is a paid OpenAI Images call on every attempt, including
  retries. Check your OpenAI pricing and usage.
- `gpt-6-luna` is the default metadata model and `gpt-image-2.5-flare` the
  default image model. If your account does not have access, change the model
  in **Settings → Publishing** to one your account can use.

### Caption presets

In **Review**, **Subtitles** turns burned captions on or off. **Framing → Fit +
blurred background** keeps the whole source image visible inside the selected
aspect ratio. **Auto** returns to automatic framing; after a manual trim it uses
a centred crop. These choices are saved per clip and used by the export.
Save transcript corrections before trimming, changing clips or exporting.
Editing controls are locked while saves and exports are in progress.

Choose **Karaoke Lower** or **Bold Pop Lower** for ready-made captions near the
bottom of the frame. Selecting a preset restores its own layout, clearing any
manual caption layout override. Opening an existing project preserves its look.

**Advanced / legacy caption layout** retains manual size and position controls.
Choose **Apply caption layout** to save and update the preview, **Discard caption
layout** to restore saved values, or **Reset caption layout** to use the preset.
Size is a percentage of video height; position uses the caption block's bottom
centre. Apply or discard pending layout changes before switching clips or
exporting. The browser preview is approximate; MP4 uses the saved ASS layout.

Choose **Manual** to move the crop over the full source image and zoom from
1× to 4×. Dragging saves once on release; keyboard sliders use **Apply framing**.
The draft rectangle is approximate until applied; the main player shows the
saved crop used for export. **Discard framing changes** restores saved values.

Choose **Per-slice framing** to use different crops within one clip. Pause at a
cut, choose **Split slice at playhead**, select a slice, then choose its Auto,
Fit or Manual framing. **Merge with next slice** keeps the selected slice's
framing. Cuts stay anchored to the source when trimming and expanding again.
Choosing a global framing mode replaces the slices.

**Render exact preview** renders up to three seconds from the playhead using the
saved FFmpeg/ASS settings. Save or discard drafts first. This preview has the
export's framing and caption rendering; it does not add an export to history.
Only one preview renders at a time, with a two-minute timeout.

| style | look |
|---|---|
| `karaoke_lower` | word-timed green karaoke near the bottom |
| `bold_pop_lower` | bold word-pop captions near the bottom |
| `bold_pop` | chunky white, heavy outline, spoken word grows and turns yellow |
| `karaoke_fill` | words fill with colour exactly as they're spoken |
| `clean_lower` | minimal lower third, no animation |
| `boxed` | high-contrast text on a solid block |

Fonts are bundled under the SIL Open Font License, so nothing is fetched at runtime.

## Troubleshooting

Everything here is a real failure hit during development, not hypothetical.

**`autoclip doctor` says Python is wrong.** You're on 3.13. MediaPipe has no wheels for it. `uv venv --python 3.11`.

**Transcription fails with "Library cublas64_12.dll is not found".** The CUDA runtime libraries aren't installed. `uv pip install -e ".[gpu]"`. AutoClip registers their location itself — pip installs them somewhere the OS loader doesn't search, which is why the error is so unhelpful.

**"Requested int8_float16 compute type, but the target device does not support..."** Clear `whisper.compute_type` in `~/.autoclip/config.json` and let AutoClip choose. It queries the backend for what's actually supported rather than guessing from your GPU model.

**Captions don't appear in exports, or ffmpeg says "No such filter: ass".** Your ffmpeg has no libass. `doctor` reports this and prints the right command for your platform. On macOS that means `brew install ffmpeg-full`, not `brew install ffmpeg`; on Windows the full Gyan build, not "essentials".

**YouTube downloads fail with a bot check.** As of 2026, YouTube blocks most anonymous downloads and proof-of-origin tokens no longer clear it. Set **Settings → Ingest → cookies from browser** to a browser you're signed into, and **close that browser** first — it locks its cookie database while running. Uploading a file always works and needs none of this.

**Exports are slower than expected.** Check `doctor` for GPU encoding. A build can list `h264_nvenc` and still be unusable if your driver is older than the NVENC API it was compiled against; AutoClip probes this and falls back to CPU encoding, which is identical quality and just slower.

**No sound in the review player.** Click **Test audio** under the player. It measures the actual signal leaving the video element and tells you whether the problem is in AutoClip or between your browser and your speakers.

## How it works

```
ingest → prepare → transcribe → highlights → reframe → captions → export
```

Each stage writes artifacts to `~/.autoclip/work/{job_id}/`, so a retry resumes at the stage that failed rather than starting over. Two decisions carry most of the design:

**Highlight detection returns word indices, not timestamps.** Models are unreliable at arithmetic and completely reliable at copying a number they can see. Timing is looked up from measured word timings afterwards.

**Crop paths never interpolate across a cut.** Shots are detected first and framed independently. Panning through an edit is the most obvious sign of an auto-reframed video.

[ARCHITECTURE.md](https://github.com/Kamil-Krawiec/autoclip/blob/main/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md) covers the rest, including why several odd-looking choices exist.

## What has and hasn't been verified

**Has been:** the full pipeline on real talking-head footage, asserted end to end — 1080×1920 h264/yuv420p output, AAC at −14 LUFS, crop segments tiling each clip without gaps, captions burned in, framing correctly following a cut to a second speaker. Run it yourself:

```bash
AUTOCLIP_E2E_MEDIA=/path/to/clip.mp4 pytest -m e2e
```

**Hasn't been:** the §6.4 reframe acceptance bar — no visible jitter, no cut-off faces, speaker on screen ≥95% of speaking time — against a fixed three-video golden set. The mechanics have unit coverage, but "looks right on footage I picked" isn't the bar. This is the gate for v0.1.0.

Also unverified: whether the clip *picks* are good. That's a judgement call about your material and your model, and no test settles it.

## Non-goals

Deliberate, not oversights:

- **No direct posting by default** to TikTok/Instagram/YouTube. The optional
  **Publish** page uploads to YouTube only after you connect your own OAuth
  credentials; AutoClip never ships credentials or posts on its own.
- **No cloud version, no accounts, no telemetry.**
- **No timeline editor** beyond trim handles and caption edits.
- **No DRM circumvention**, ever.

## Contributing

Prompts and caption styles are the highest-leverage places to start, and neither needs deep knowledge of the codebase — `backend/autoclip/prompts/highlight_v1.txt` is plain text and affects output quality more than most code changes. See [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/Kamil-Krawiec/autoclip/blob/main/docs/CONTRIBUTING.md).

## Legal

AutoClip bundles [yt-dlp](https://github.com/yt-dlp/yt-dlp). **Only download content you own or have the rights to process.** AutoClip contains no workarounds for DRM or paywalled content and never will.

